Done. The farewell path now has a server-validated message library, and the live stage renders and speaks whichever message the operator picks.

## What changed

**New library file** — [config/farewell-messages.json](config/farewell-messages.json) registers the existing message as both the default and the only entry: id `until-we-meet-again`, four body paragraphs plus the closing "God bless you. Amen." Nothing in it is editable from the browser.

**Server** ([server.js](server.js)) — the library is normalised once at boot (missing, malformed or duplicate ids fall back to the built-in copy). `show.farewell` now accepts `{ messageId }`:
- no id → the registered default, so the existing End Live button behaves exactly as before;
- unknown id → refused with `Unknown farewell message "…"` and never reaches the stage;
- a farewell already on screen → refused with the existing single-playback lock, for every message;
- the cue, the `show.farewell` payload and the `tts.play` text are all built from the same library entry, so what the stage draws and what Piper reads cannot differ.

New RPC `show.farewellList` returns only sanitised fields (id, title, eyebrow, paragraphs, closing, text, character count) — no filesystem paths.

**Admin** ([public/admin.html](public/admin.html), [public/js/admin.js](public/js/admin.js), [public/css/admin.css](public/css/admin.css)) — a *Farewell Message Library* block sits directly under the End Live button: each message shows its title, paragraph count ("Default · 4 paragraphs"), a collapsible *Show message text*, and its own **Play on Live Stage**. All farewell buttons share one disabled state, and the status line names the message being played (`“Until We Meet Again” playing on the Live Stage (45s)`). Refusals re-enable the controls with the server's reason.

**Stage** ([public/js/live.js](public/js/live.js), [public/css/live.css](public/css/live.css)) — the same `#farewellScene` overlay renders paragraphs dynamically from the payload; a fit pass shrinks copy and title in steps until a long blessing fits the 1080×1920 frame. Design, ducking, auto-hide after narration and the cue/quiz suppression are unchanged.

[README.md](README.md) documents the library and adds `show.farewellList` to the RPC table.

## Tests actually executed

Isolated instance on port 3120; `data/prayer-state.json` backed up before and restored after.

- `node --check` on `server.js`, `live.js`, `admin.js`, `audio.js` — all OK; library JSON parses.
- **Library API suite: 21/21** — list returns the default message with 4 paragraphs + closing and 418-char text; unknown id rejected; default path (`show.farewell` with no id) still works; returned text is byte-identical to the library text; cue carries 5 paragraphs; duplicate and second-message requests both refused while active; the stage received `started`/`speaking` with the right `messageId` and text; `tts.play` carried the identical text (20.05 s, `en_US-amy-medium`); background, music volume, TikTok connection and programme state all unchanged.
- **Temporary second message** (8 paragraphs, 1,206 chars, then removed): the library listed both, selecting it produced title *A Longer Test Blessing*, 8 paragraphs + closing on the stage, `ttsPlaying: true`, admin hint `“A Longer Test Blessing” playing on the Live Stage (45s)` with both button sets disabled. Fit check in the real page: card height 1699 px against 1728 px available after one shrink step (copy 30 → 28.5 px, title 74 → 71.5 px), no overflow, no console errors.
- **Refactored End Live button** re-verified in the browser after the change: it plays the default, shows its title, and disables the library buttons too.
- Existing suites: **smoke 168/168**, **reliability 77/77**. (My first smoke attempt failed because Piper was still loading 8 s after boot; the rerun once the voice worker was ready passed in full.)

## Limitations

- Only the existing message is registered — adding another means editing the JSON and restarting (the library is read once at startup, so the browser can never inject text).
- Narration still depends on the stage's audio being unlocked in OBS, as with every other Piper message.
- The saved screenshot in `docs/screenshots/farewell-stage.png` is the default message from the previous step; the long-message render was inspected live but not saved to disk.

Cleanup: test server stopped, `data/prayer-state.json`, `data/health-state.json`, `config/schedule.backup.json` and both music manifests restored to their committed contents, temporary test scripts and logs removed, no production server touched (nothing was listening on 3000). No commits made.

The library is now the natural place to grow the closing content — a short "technical difficulties" sign-off and a "back in a moment" notice would use the same validated path.
